    "text": "{\"anomaly_detected\": true, \"confidence\": 0.85, \"anomalies\": [{\"category\": \"bridged_nets\", \"components\": [\"J3\", \"U6\", \"U25\", \"R11\", \"R64\", \"Q1\"], \"pins\": [\"J3.A4\", \"J3.A9\", \"J3.B4\", \"J3.B9\", \"J3.A7\", \"J3.B7\", \"U6.1\", \"U25.5\", \"U25.4\", \"R64.2\"], \"nets\": [\"USBC_D_N\"], \"observed\": \"The net USBC_D_N contains the USB-C VBUS pins (J3.A4, J3.A9, J3.B4, J3.B9) together with the USB-C D- data pins (J3.A7, J3.B7). It also carries the LTC4412 input U6.1(VIN), the USBLC6 VBUS pin U25.5, the P-MOSFET drain Q1.3, and the pull-up/pull-down network R11 and R64. VBUS and D- are therefore the same node.\", \"expected\": \"USB-C VBUS (5 V input to the LTC4412 ideal-diode path) and the USB D- data line must be separate nets. Only VBUS should feed U6.1, Q1.3 and U25.5, and D- should connect to U25.4, R11 and R64 only through the data path.\", \"evidence\": [{\"source\": \"netlist\", \"quote\": \"USBC_D_N : J3.A4(VBUS,passive) J3.A7(D-,bidirectional) J3.A9(VBUS,passive) J3.B4(VBUS,passive) J3.B7(D-,bidirectional) J3.B9(VBUS,passive) Q1.3(D,passive) R11.1(passive) R64.2(2,passive) U25.4(I/O2,passive) U25.5(VBUS,passive) U6.1(VIN,power_in)\"}, {\"source\": \"spec\", \"quote\": \"USB input \\\"OR-ing\\\"/protection circuitry (LTC4412 ideal-diode controller, `U6`).\"}], \"impact\": \"The USB-C data lines are shorted to the 5 V/VBUS input. This would drive the D+/D- pair to VBUS voltage, damage the USB data transceiver/ESD parts, and prevent proper USB enumeration. It also defeats the LTC4412 power-path function.\", \"confidence\": 0.85}, {\"category\": \"wrong_value\", \"components\": [\"R17\", \"R16\", \"U12\", \"C19\"], \"pins\": [\"R17.1\", \"R17.2\", \"R16.1\", \"U12.3\"], \"nets\": [\"FB\", \"+12V\"], \"observed\": \"R17 (1.8 M\u03a9) runs from FB to +12V and R16 (205 k\u03a9) runs from FB to GND. With the TPS61040 feedback threshold of about 200 mV, this divider sets the output to roughly 0.2 \u00d7 (1 + 1.8M/205k) \u2248 2 V, not 12 V.\", \"expected\": \"For a +12 V output with a 205 k\u03a9 bottom resistor, the top resistor should be about 12 M\u03a9 (0.2 \u00d7 (1 + R17/205k) = 12 V).\", \"evidence\": [{\"source\": \"netlist\", \"quote\": \"R17 | 1.8M | Resistor_SMD:R_0603_1608Metric | Device:R | Resistor | LCSC=C403036\"}, {\"source\": \"netlist\", \"quote\": \"FB : C19.2(passive) R16.2(passive) R17.1(passive) U12.3(FB,passive)\"}, {\"source\": \"spice\", \"quote\": \"R17 FB +12V 1.8Meg\"}], \"impact\": \"If the value is wrong, the +12 V boost rail would regulate to about 2 V, leaving any +12 V-powered load unpowered or damaged.\", \"confidence\": 0.3}], \"summary\": \"The netlist places USB-C VBUS pins and the USB D- data pins on the same net (USBC_D_N), which shorts the data lines to the 5 V input. This is the most likely anomaly. A secondary suspicion is that the 1.8 M\u03a9 top feedback resistor of the +12 V boost divider does not produce 12 V.\"}",
